      "id": "d38198c6aed0b3f3ad884d8be5957ac6a723beedf1fa02e7e1139ff76e55d2c9",
      "title": "IX-WAI Early Career Development Grant 2027 (up to £1,000)",
      "url": "https://opportunitydesk.org/2026/11/05/ix-wai-early-career-development-grant-2027",
      "category": "opportunities",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T06:35:08.112Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/ix-ai-in-science/i-x-women-in-ai-network/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "I-X Women in AI Network",
      "pageDescription": "Find out more about I-X Women in AI Network (IX-WAI)",
      "evidenceText": "I-X Women in AI Network | Research groups | Imperial College London Skip to main content View accessibility support page In this section Imperial Home Research groups I-X Centre for AI in Science I-X Women in AI Network I-X Women in AI Network I-X Centre for AI in Science Our vision and fellowship Our People AI in Science Fellowships Events Resources I-X Women in AI Network Eric and Wendy Schmidt AI in Science Global Faculty Fellowships Contact us AI in Science and Lab Automation Awards for Imperial Staff Mission statement At the I-X Women in Artificial Intelligence (IX-WAI) network , we are dedicated to empowering women, non-binary people and other gender-discriminated groups* in the field of artificial intelligence. IX-WAI aims to create a supportive and inclusive network that champions career advancement, addresses challenges, and fosters connections across all career stages. Our mission is to enhance representation of women in AI and foster an environment where they feel valued, supported, and inspired to achieve their full potential. We intend to address these by: Inviting speakers to spotlight women&rsquo;s achievements and their career journey in the &lsquo;Cutting-Edge Overviews & Tutorials&rsquo; and the &lsquo;AI: Ethics and Diversity&rsquo; seminar series; Hosting workshops and round panels to offer platforms for discussion on issues related to improving the work culture; Creating and showcasing an inclusive work culture that encourages other women to join the community; Providing opportunities for mentorship, learning, and collaboration. Our monthly meetings are open to all women of the Digital Foundry, aimed at devising strategies for future initiatives and ensuring our actions align with our core belief: that every woman in AI deserves a supportive community. By increasing visibility and advocating for women&rsquo;s voices in AI, we strive to correct the underrepresentation and build a future where diversity drives innovation. The IX-WAI network is a movement towards a more equitable, dynamic, and inclusive AI landscape within the Digital Foundry, Imperial and the AI field. *For readability, we will refer to all of these groups as &ldquo;women&rdquo; in the following text. IX-WAI Early Career Development Grant The I-X Women in AI Network (IX-WAI) provides Early Career Development Grants of up to &pound;1,000 to women and non-binary individuals who are currently working in AI or wish to enter the field. These grants are intended to support conference attendance, workshops, training, and other professional development opportunities. This initiative reflects our commitment to providing additional support for women and non-binary individuals in AI. Call for Round 7: Opening date: 00:00, Monday 2 November 2026 Closing date: 23:45, Monday 4 January 2027 The application process consists of two stages. For the pre-selection stage, applicants must complete the online form, providing all the information required to justify the requested budget and explain the purpose and expected impact of the proposed activity. Shortlisted applicants will then be invited to a 15 minute online interview (expected to take place in last week of January), consisting of a 5 minute presentation followed by 10 minutes of questions from the panel. For enquiries regarding the grant, please contact s.veneziale21@imperial.ac.uk or r.birolo@imperial.ac.uk . Please note that questions already addressed on the website will not receive a response. Eligibility How To Apply What Do We Support? Criteria for Preselection Use of Generative AI in Applications Funding Rounds and Deadlines Post-Award Requirements Free Resources for Getting Started with AI Eligibility The grant is open to women and non-binary individuals at Master's, PhD, or postdoctoral level at Imperial College London who are interested in learning about AI or are currently working in the field. Please note: Applicants who have already been awarded this grant are not eligible to apply again. How To Apply To apply for a grant during the pre-selection stage, applicants must complete a brief online form and provide the following information: Requested amount: Up to &pound;1,000, with a fully justified budget detailing the expected costs (e.g., conference registration fees, workshop fees, travel, and other expenses). If the maximum budget of &pound;1,000 does not cover all the expenses for the activity, please justify how you will cover the remaining costs. Departmental account number (please enter your supervisor&rsquo;s/mentor&rsquo;s departmental F account number for the fund transfer). Purpose of the grant: A one-sentence summary of the proposed activity and its purpose. Brief proposal (up to 300 words), including: 1. A clear description of the planned activity. 2. The main motivation for participating in the activity. Expected impact on career development (up to 100 words), describing the anticipated learning outcomes and how the activity will contribute to your research and professional growth. Curriculum Vitae (CV) Apply here The application form will open at 00:00 on Monday 2 November 2026 and will close at 23:45 on Monday 4 January 2027. Applicants who successfully pass the pre-selection stage will be invited to the interview stage, where they will be expected to present their proposal through a PowerPoint presentation. What Do We Support? The grant can cover registration fees and travel expenses for conferences, workshops, tutorials, training sessions, and other activities that enable applicants to present their research, expand their professional networks, and develop new skills in AI. AI is interpreted broadly and may encompass a range of fields, including modelling and automation, as well as AI for scientific applications (e.g., in chemistry, biology, mathematics, physics, engineering, etc.) We generally do not cover the costs of online courses or resources that can be freely available from other sources (see a list below). Applicants seeking funding for an online course are strongly encouraged to provide a clear justification explaining why this option represents the best for their career development. The proposal should highlight the value of any certification obtained and clarify that equivalent free resources or in-person courses are not available. Criteria for Preselection The proposed activity should have AI at its core, including its applications in scientific research and related disciplines. The proposed activity should demonstrate a meaningful impact on the applicant's academic, research or professional development. The skills and competencies to be developed through the activity should be clearly identified. Timing and career development: Why is this activity particularly relevant at this stage of the applicant&rsquo;s career? Why is now the right time to pursue it? Are there alternative funding opportunities available to the applicant? Knowledge sharing and alignment with WAI&rsquo;s vision. Use of Generative AI in Applications Applicants must not use generative AI tools to generate the application, neither to write the initial proposal from scratch, neither formulate the personal motivation and expected career impact of the proposed activity. The ideas and personal reflections presented in the application must originate from the applicant. Generative AI may be used only for limited purposes, including: Translating an application from another language into English. Improving the standard of English. Formatting the application or reducing its word count. Applicants must not use generative AI tools to prepare their interview presentation slides. Funding Rounds and Deadlines We will launch two calls for applications each year, awarding four grants annually. Please note: the activity for which you are applying must take place   at least two months after   the application deadline. The awarded funding cannot be used retrospectively. The IX-WAI will review applications and make decisions once a term. Post-Award Requirements Recipients of the mini grants will be required to provide confirmation of attendance and a brief feedback paragraph about their experience. This feedback will be published on our website to inspire and inform others. Free Resources for Getting Started with AI If you&rsquo;re looking for online courses to learn the fundamentals of AI and deep learning modelling, you can find a brief list of free learning resources below. ECRI offers a series of courses covering the fundamentals of Python and introducing key concepts in machine learning and deep learning: Managing and Running Python Effectively Machine Learning with Python Deep Learning with Python Online Tutorials: Tutorials from the Deep learning course of the University of Amsterdam , covering several deep learning architectures and their practical implementations in PyTorch. Learn PyTorch : practical tutorials and examples for learning PyTorch Distill : visual and simplified technical explanations of different machine learning concepts. Other Resources: Coursera access through Imperial Additional Resources from IX Imperial Lifelong Learning offers professional development opportunities, including courses in AI and related disciplines. AI for the Workplace is new learning programme designed to develop AI literacy and use AI effectively and responsibly. The first course, AI Foundations for Professional Contexts , is now available. You can also find additional support and courses for your career development here . Past Events Past Events The events we organised and/or supported in the past: 10/06/2026: Women in Science Career Day 11/06/2025: Women in Science Career Day 03/12/2024: AI: Cutting-Edge Overview and Tutorials with Linda Wen  18/07/2024: AI: Cutting-Edge Overviews and Tutorial Series with Professor Claudia Clopath 16/07/2024: Managing the Impostor Phenomenon workshop by Dr Tracy Bussoli   20/06/2024: AI: Ethics & Diversity Seminar Series with Professor Lesley Cohen   23/05/2024: AI: Ethics & Diversity Seminar Series with Professor Michael Huth   09/05/2024: AI: Ethics & Diversity Seminar Series with Dr Mary Meaney Haynes   I-X WAI Committee Claire Baker Personal details Claire Baker Eric and Wendy Schmidt AI in Science Postdoctoral Fellow Eileen Boyce Personal details Eileen Boyce Manager of I-X Centre for AI in Science Gema Vera-Gonzalez Personal details Gema Vera-Gonzalez Eric and Wendy Schmidt AI in Science Postdoctoral Fellow & Alumni Irene Mendez Guerra Personal details Irene Mendez Guerra Eric and Wendy Schmidt AI in Science Postdoctoral Fellow Laura Helleckes Personal details Laura Helleckes Eric and Wendy Schmidt AI in Science Postdoctoral Fellow Mili Ostojic Personal details Mili Ostojic Postgraduate Researcher (Life Sciences & I-X) Rebecca Birolo Personal details Rebecca Birolo Eric and Wendy Schmidt AI in Science Postdoctoral Fellow Sara Veneziale Personal details Sara Veneziale Research Fellowship (AI), Imperial Policy Forum Tatiana Codreanu Personal details Tatiana Codreanu Part-time Teaching Fellow (Adult Education) Tracy Bussoli Personal details Tracy Bussoli Professional Development Consultant for AI in Science Fellows Get in touch For enquiries, please contact: Eileen Boyce e.boyce@imperial.ac.uk For Global Faculty Fellowships: GFF@imperial.ac.uk",
