      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://opportunitydesk.org/2026/11/07/how-to-get-a-scholarship-with-a-third-class-degree-7-practical-steps-to-improve-your-chances/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "How to Get a Scholarship with a Third-Class Degree: 7 Practical Steps to Improve Your Chances",
      "pageDescription": "",
      "evidenceText": "How to Get a Scholarship with a Third-Class Degree: 7 Practical Steps to Improve Your Chances – Opportunity Desk Close Menu Home Conferences Training Scholarships Undergraduate Short Courses Online Courses Masters/Postgraduate PhD Postdoctoral Study Abroad Study in Africa Study in Asia Study in Australia Study in Europe Study in USA Competitions Awards Grants Fellowships Jobs Internships Volunteering Blog OD Specials OD Live Series Young Person of the Month Mentorship OD Mentors Success Stories How-To General Tips ODIC 2026 ODIC 2026 Application ODIC Judges ODIC 2025 Winners ODIC 2023 Winners ODIC 2022 Winners ODIC 2021 Winners ODIC 2019 Winners ODIC 2018 Winners ODIC 2017 Winners ODIC 2016 Winners Search Asia Africa Australia and Oceania America Europe Deadlines Approaching Facebook X (Twitter) Instagram Monday, November 16 Facebook X (Twitter) LinkedIn Instagram Telegram How to Get a Scholarship with a Third-Class Degree: 7 Practical Steps to Improve Your Chances Favour Abatang November 7, 2026 6 Mins Read Facebook Twitter WhatsApp Pinterest LinkedIn Tumblr Email Telegram Graduating with a third-class degree can feel discouraging, especially when you begin searching for scholarships and discover that many opportunities require a first-class or second-class upper degree. For some graduates, this becomes a reason to abandon their dreams of further education. However, your undergraduate result does not have to determine how far you can go academically or professionally. While a third-class degree may limit your eligibility for certain scholarships, it does not automatically disqualify you from every opportunity. Some scholarship programmes consider professional experience, leadership potential, community service, financial need, personal achievements and other evidence of your ability to succeed. If you graduated with a third-class degree and still dream of pursuing postgraduate education, here are seven practical steps to improve your chances. 1. Look for scholarships that consider more than academic grades Not every scholarship is awarded solely on academic excellence. Some programmes prioritise leadership, community development, professional achievements, financial need or contributions to society. Begin by researching scholarships that assess applicants holistically rather than relying exclusively on undergraduate grades. Pay close attention to the eligibility criteria, as some scholarships have strict minimum degree classifications while others offer more flexible pathways. For example, the Chevening Scholarship places significant emphasis on leadership, networking and professional experience. However, applicants must also meet its academic eligibility requirements and qualify for admission to an eligible UK master’s programme. The goal is to identify opportunities where your overall profile can be considered, rather than repeatedly applying for scholarships whose minimum academic requirements you do not meet. 2. Build relevant professional experience If your academic record is not particularly strong, professional experience can help demonstrate your abilities, commitment and potential. Consider gaining experience through employment, internships, volunteering, research assistance, community projects or entrepreneurship. Focus on opportunities connected to the field you intend to study. For example, if you want to pursue a master’s degree in international development, experience working with a nonprofit organisation, implementing community programmes or supporting development initiatives could strengthen your application. More importantly, document your achievements. Rather than simply stating that you volunteered for an organisation, explain what you contributed, the responsibilities you handled and the results you helped achieve. Your experience should provide convincing evidence that you have developed the knowledge, skills and maturity needed for postgraduate study. 3. Strengthen your academic profile with additional qualifications A third-class degree is part of your academic history, but it does not have to be the last evidence of your academic ability. Consider taking relevant professional certifications, accredited courses, postgraduate diplomas or other recognised qualifications that can demonstrate academic improvement. For some students, completing a postgraduate diploma or qualifying programme may provide a pathway into a master’s degree, depending on the university’s admission requirements. You can also explore universities that offer pre-master’s programmes or alternative entry routes for applicants whose undergraduate qualifications do not meet standard admission requirements. Before enrolling in any programme, confirm that the qualification is recognised and that it can genuinely improve your eligibility for your preferred university or scholarship. 4. Write a compelling personal statement Your personal statement is an opportunity to tell your story beyond your academic transcript. Scholarship committees want to understand your motivation, achievements, career ambitions and the contribution you hope to make through further education. If there were circumstances that affected your undergraduate performance, you may explain them briefly and honestly. However, your statement should not become a lengthy justification for your grades. Instead, focus on what you have learned, how you have grown and what you have accomplished since graduation. Explain why you are interested in your chosen programme, what makes you prepared for it and how the scholarship will help you achieve meaningful goals. Most importantly, demonstrate your potential through real experiences and concrete achievements. A strong personal statement does not erase a weak academic record, but it can help evaluators understand the person behind the grades. 5. Apply to universities with flexible admission pathways One mistake many scholarship applicants make is focusing entirely on funding without first considering whether they qualify for admission to their preferred universities. Some universities consider applicants with lower degree classifications when they have substantial relevant professional experience or additional qualifications. Others provide alternative pathways, including postgraduate diplomas, qualifying programmes or pre-master’s courses. Research institutions that assess applicants based on a combination of academic qualifications, work experience and other relevant achievements. You can also contact university admissions offices directly to explain your qualifications and ask whether alternative entry options are available. Remember that university admission and scholarship eligibility are separate decisions. Even if a university accepts your application, you must still meet the requirements of the scholarship you intend to pursue. 6. Secure strong recommendation letters A strong recommendation letter can help demonstrate your capabilities beyond your undergraduate results. Approach people who know your work well, including employers, supervisors, lecturers, project coordinators or professional mentors, depending on the scholarship’s requirements. Choose referees who can provide specific examples of your leadership, problem-solving abilities, work ethic, academic potential and contributions. For example, a supervisor who has worked closely with you on a successful project may be able to explain how you managed responsibilities, overcame challenges or delivered measurable results. Avoid choosing referees solely because they hold prestigious positions. A detailed recommendation from someone who genuinely understands your strengths is often more useful than a generic letter from a prominent individual. 7. Apply strategically and remain consistent Securing a scholarship with a third-class degree may require additional preparation, careful research and persistence. Rather than submitting applications indiscriminately, develop a targeted scholarship strategy. Identify programmes that match your qualifications, professional background and career goals. Review their eligibility requirements, deadlines, required documents and selection criteria. Where possible, attend scholarship webinars, connect with previous recipients and seek feedback on your application materials. If you receive a rejection, use the experience to identify areas for improvement. You may need to strengthen your professional experience, improve your personal statement, gain additional qualifications or explore a different academic pathway. Consistency matters, but so does strategy. Applying to scholarships for which you are genuinely eligible will give you a better chance than submitting numerous applications that do not meet the basic requirements. Final thoughts A third-class degree may make your scholarship journey more challenging, but it does not mean your academic dreams are over. Your grades reflect one period of your educational journey. They do not capture every skill you have developed, every challenge you have overcome or every contribution you are capable of making. What matters now is how intentionally you prepare for the opportunities ahead. Build your experience, strengthen your qualifications, research flexible admission pathways and develop applications that demonstrate your potential. Most importantly, be realistic about eligibility requirements while remaining open to alternative routes. Your degree classification is part of your story, but it does not have to be the end of your story. For more scholarship opportunities, application tips and educational resources, visit Opportunity Desk and explore opportunities that match your qualifications and aspirations. Share. Facebook Twitter WhatsApp LinkedIn Email Pinterest Telegram Previous Article Arewa Creative Writing and Advocacy Fellowship (ACWAF) 2026 – Cohort 1 Next Article How to Turn Your Passion into Income: 5 Practical Ways to Make Money Doing What You Love Favour Abatang Facebook X (Twitter) Instagram LinkedIn Favour Abatang is a Non-profit Executive and International Development Expert with experience leading an organisation, curating programs, and fundraising. She has made significant strides in supporting teenage mothers and at-risk girls through tailored second-chance opportunities. She is currently expanding her impact as the Community Manager at Opportunity Desk. Related Posts Morris Animal Foundation Veterinary Student Scholar Program 2026 ($5,500 stipend) November 14, 2026 UCD Iseult Honohan Doctoral Scholarship 2027 (Fully-funded) November 13, 2026 Closes in 60 days E-JUST TICAD9 African Scholarship for STI – Fall 2027 November 12, 2026 Search for: Receive Opportunity Alerts! Join over 100,000 email subscribers to receive weekly summary of opportunities. Sign up here . OD YPOM – Joy Nwaeze Joy Nwaeze is OD Young Person of the Month – November 2026. Read more . Latest Posts BusinessDay Young Economists Writing Challenge 2026 (N450,000 in prizes) Morris Animal Foundation Veterinary Student Scholar Program 2026 ($5,500 stipend) Ocean Futures Fellowship 2027: Building Ocean Career Pathways Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (UN FAO) Award for Innovation 2027 ($10,000 prize) Council of Europe Grants for Civil Society Organisations 2026 (up to €20,000) UCD Iseult Honohan Doctoral Scholarship 2027 (Fully-funded) Bayer Foundation Scientific Fellowships 2027 (up to €10,000) US-UK Fulbright Commission UK Summer Institutes 2027 (Funded) Follow on Twitter Tweets by @OpportunityDesk Follow Us on Social Media Submit Type above and press Enter to search. Press Esc to cancel. Ad Blocker Enabled! Our website is made possible by displaying online advertisements to our visitors. Please support us by disabling your Ad Blocker.",
