      "pageTitle": "Wave Research",
      "pageDescription": "Wave Research — Wave Winter Research Fellowship (WWRF) is a free 8-week scientific research and project incubator (Nov 15, 2026 – Jan 15, 2027) preparing students for Sabahın Alimləri and Regeneron ISEF. Extended deadline: Nov 15, 2026 (rolling late admissions through Nov 20, 2026).",
      "evidenceText": "Wave Research (WWRF) | Sabahın Alimləri & Regeneron ISEF (ICEF) Research Fellowship Cohort 1 Onboarding: Nov 15, 2026 | Extended Application Deadline: Nov 15, 2026 (Rolling Late Admissions Open Through Nov 20, 2026) Wave Winter Research Fellowship 8-Week Student Research & Project Incubator — Baku In-Person & International Online Pathways (Nov 15, 2026 – Jan 15, 2027). An 8-week scientific research and project incubator guiding students from theoretical ideas to functional outcomes—featuring Pathway 1 (Baku In-Person), Pathway 2 (International Online Mentorship & Defense), and optional Azerbaijan teammate matching. Student Registration (AZ) Student Registration (EN / International) Mentor Recruitment View 8-Week Schedule 20+ Countries USA, India, EU, CIS & Worldwide 5 STEM Tracks ISEF-Aligned Divisions 8 Weeks Intensive R&D Incubator 100% Free $0 Fee · Mentorship & Journal From Research Hypothesis to Stand or Online Oral Defense Mentored by Regeneron ISEF & International Olympiad Winners 2026–2027 Cohort Schedule 08 d 00 h 00 m 00 s left (late: Nov 20) Stages 1–2: Plan & Prototype 1-page plan (Nov 28) & Mid-Term review (by Dec 19) Nov 15 – Dec 19 Stage 3: Final Paper Before Break Complete practical work & paper before Dec 27 – Jan 4 break Dec 20 – 26 Stage 4: Presentation Prep Mentor revisions & mock pitch rehearsal Jan 5 – 8 Stages 5–6: Defense & Wave Journal Baku stand or Online oral defense (Jan 9) & Top 10 / Awards (Jan 15) Jan 9 & Jan 15 Extended Deadline Nov 15 (Rolling Through Nov 20) Cohort 1 Onboarding & Dates November 15 – January 15 Stage 2 & Mid-Term Nov 29 – Dec 19 Final Exhibition & Defense Jan 9 (Baku & Online) Participation Tracks · Local & International Two Fellowship Pathways & Joint Azerbaijan Matching Whether you are based in Azerbaijan or applying as an international student from abroad, you receive the full 8-week mentorship and compete for the same Wave Journal Top 10 publication slots. Pathway 1 · In-Person (Baku) National Track (Azerbaijan) For students based in Azerbaijan attending the live stand exhibition in Baku on January 9, 2027. Defense Format: In-person stand & oral defense in Baku (Jan 9) Wave Journal: Competes for Top 10 publication Rec Letters: Eligible after oral defense Awards: Full eligibility for competition awards Pathway 2 · International Online International Mentorship & Online Defense For international students worldwide who complete the 8-week mentorship and present their project at the Online Exhibition & Oral Defense. Defense Format: Online exhibition & live oral defense (Jan 9) Wave Journal: Competes for the exact same Top 10 journal slots Rec Letters: Professor recommendation letters awarded after oral defense Awards Policy: Non-award track (no physical competition prizes) Hybrid Option · Cross-Border Team Match with a Student in Azerbaijan International applicants can request to be matched with a co-author in Azerbaijan so their joint team can defend in person at the Baku Exhibition. Defense Format: Azerbaijani teammate defends at the Baku stand (Jan 9) Wave Journal: Competes for Top 10 publication Rec Letters: Eligible for all co-authors after defense Awards: Unlocks full eligibility for competition awards Program Overview Program Structure & Academic Outcomes Structured mentorship, weekly progress tracking, and formal research documentation for national and international science competitions. ISEF & Olympiad Mentors Work weekly in small teams with winners and alumni of Regeneron ISEF, \"Sabahın Alimləri\", and international STEM olympiads. Wave Journal Publication Both Pathway 1 (Baku) and Pathway 2 (International Online) teams compete for the exact same Top 10 publication slots in the Wave Journal with full student and mentor credit. Professor Recommendations Distinguished participants in both pathways (after completing the in-person or online oral defense) earn official recommendation letters from university professors. Competition-Ready Output Graduate with a tested prototype, codebase, or proof and a comprehensive research paper (no page limits; submit as detailed as needed) tailored to ISEF & Sabahın Alimləri standards. 5 Research Tracks Choose Your Scientific Division Select any division to review its Stage 1 Plan (Nov 28), Stage 2 Mid-Term (Dec 19), and Stage 3 Final (Dec 26) deliverables. Compare Track Milestones → 01 · MATH Mathematics Formal proofs, algorithmic bounds, number theory, or applied mathematical modeling. Milestones → 02 · SOFT / AI Computer Science & AI Working software, machine learning models tested on real datasets, and baseline benchmarks. Milestones → 03 · ENMC / PHYS Engineering & Physics Physical prototypes, experimental apparatus, and empirical measurement validation. Milestones → 04 · BCHM / CHEM Natural Sciences (Biology & Chemistry) Controlled laboratory experiments, repeated trials alongside control groups, and data analysis. Milestones → 05 · EBED / ROBO Hardware & Robotics Electronic circuits, embedded systems, and autonomous robotic mechanisms tested in practice. Milestones → Structured Schedule How the 8-Week Fellowship Works Designed around the school calendar so teams finish paper writing before winter break and focus on presentation after Jan 5. Required Documents → Weeks 1–2 · Nov 15–28 1. Problem & Plan Match with your mentor (and optionally pair with an Azerbaijani co-author if applying internationally), define a concrete research question, and submit your 1-page Project Plan by November 28. Weeks 3–5 · Nov 29–Dec 19 2. Prototype & Mid-Term Build your initial working code, apparatus, or experimental trial and present a 3-minute progress pitch at the Mid-Term Review. Week 6 · Dec 20–26 3. Paper Before Break Finalize empirical results and complete your Final Research Paper (no page limit; submit as comprehensive as needed) right before the Dec 27 – Jan 4 winter break. Weeks 7–8 · Jan 5–15 4. Exhibition, Oral Defense & Journal Rehearse pitches on Jan 5–8, defend on Jan 9 (in-person stand in Baku or online oral defense for Pathway 2), and receive Wave Journal Top 10 & recommendation results on Jan 15. The Competition Stage · Baku & Online Defend Your Research Before a Scientific Jury (In-Person or Online) Every team that passes the Mid-Term Assessment (by December 19) and submits its Final Research Paper (no page limit; submit as detailed as needed) before winter break (December 26) advances to the January 9, 2027 Final Exhibition. Pathway 1 and Joint (AZ-matched) teams defend at physical stands in Baku; Pathway 2 international teams present live via Online Exhibition & Oral Defense. Jan 9 · Stand or Online Oral Defense Baku stand defense (Pathway 1 / Joint teams) or live online oral presentation & Q&A (Pathway 2 international students). Jan 15 · Wave Journal, Rec Letters & Awards Both pathways compete for Top 10 Wave Journal publication & Professor Recommendation Letters (competition awards reserved for Baku in-person/joint teams). About the Incubator The Wave Winter Research Fellowship (Project Incubator) empowers youth to step outside traditional theoretical frameworks, identify real-world problems, and develop practical solutions. Under the mentorship of alumni and winners of national and international science competitions (such as Regeneron ISEF), participants conduct systematic research to turn their ideas into functional outcomes (mathematical proofs, working software, physical prototypes, or experimental data). We do not ask for revolutionary discoveries; instead, we expect honest scientific methodology and creative solutions that add new value to existing problems. Eligibility, International Pathway 2 & Academic Recognition The fellowship is open to motivated students in Azerbaijan ( Pathway 1 — In-Person Track ) and international students worldwide ( Pathway 2 — Online Mentorship & Defense Track ) across five core divisions: Mathematics, Computer Science & AI, Engineering & Physics, Natural Sciences (Biology & Chemistry), and Hardware & Robotics. International students may also opt to be matched with a student from Azerbaijan so their joint team can present in person at the Baku Final Exhibition. Pathway 2 (International Online) Policy: International teams completing the online exhibition and oral defense do not receive physical competition awards, but after passing the oral defense they are fully eligible for official recommendation letters from university professors and compete directly alongside local teams for the Top 10 publication slots in the Wave Journal . International students who match with an Azerbaijani co-author for in-person defense in Baku are eligible for all competition awards as well. Eligibility & Age Structure Program Eligibility Tracks Ages 14–22 · Weekly Mentor Pods Mentored 8-Week Fellowship Open to High School (Grades 9–12) & Undergraduate Students (Ages 14–22) . Participants are placed in small weekly mentorship pods guided by Regeneron ISEF and international olympiad alumni. Ages 23+ · Peer-Review Track Independent Scholar / Wave Journal Track Open to graduate/postgraduate researchers (23+) submitting original manuscripts directly for peer review and Wave Journal consideration without requiring weekly mentor pods. Sponsorship & Institutional Partners Partners & Track Sponsors Dedicated partner and track sponsorship placements for the 2026–2027 Wave Winter Research Fellowship cohort. Partner / Sponsor Inquiry → Program-Wide Partner Exclusive Education Partner [Reserved · Partner Slot] Track 01 · Mathematics Track Sponsor [Sponsor Slot Available] Track 02 · Computer Science & AI Track Sponsor [Sponsor Slot Available] Track 03 · Engineering & Physics Track Sponsor [Sponsor Slot Available] Track 04 · Natural Sciences Track Sponsor [Sponsor Slot Available] Track 05 · Hardware & Robotics Track Sponsor [Reserved · Sponsor Slot] Ready to Build Your Research Project? Participation is 100% free for both Pathway 1 (Azerbaijan In-Person) and Pathway 2 (International Online / AZ-Matched). Extended application deadline: Nov 15, 2026 (rolling late admissions open through Nov 20, 2026). Student Registration (AZ) Student Registration (EN / International) Mentor Recruitment All Divisions Overview 01. Mathematics 02. Computer Science & AI 03. Engineering & Physics 04. Natural Sciences 05. Hardware & Robotics Division By Mid-Term Assessment (Nov 29 – Dec 19) By Final Submission (Dec 26) Mathematics &rarr; Defined problem, methodology, partial proofs, or solved baseline examples. Complete solution (formal proof) verified with examples or test cases. Computer Science & AI &rarr; Working code and preliminary tests conducted on real data. Tested functional software, measurable results, and comparison with baseline indicators. Engineering & Physics &rarr; Initial prototype or experimental apparatus where preliminary measurements have been taken. Functional prototype and comparison of obtained empirical data with the initial objective. Natural Sciences (Biology & Chemistry) &rarr; Experimental setup established and initial trial conducted. Repeated trials alongside a control group and a clear scientific conclusion. Hardware & Robotics &rarr; Circuit or robot executing at least one core function. Device achieving the stated objective along with its empirical test results. Track 01 · Pure & Applied Mathematics ← All Divisions Comparison Mathematics Covers pure and applied mathematics projects where students formulate a clear mathematical problem, investigate patterns or models, and construct a complete proof or verified analytical solution. Algebra & Number Theory Combinatorics & Graph Theory Geometry & Topology Analysis & Differential Equations Probability & Mathematical Statistics Game Theory & Optimization Stage 1 · Nov 15 – 28 1-Page Project Plan Clearly defined mathematical problem, research question or conjecture, planned method of proof or modeling, references, and expected result. Stage 2 · By Dec 19 (Mid-Term) Methodology & Partial Proofs Defined problem, chosen methodology, partial proofs, or solved baseline examples presented in a 3-minute progress review to mentors. Stage 3 · By Dec 26 (Final Paper) Verified Complete Proof & Paper Complete mathematical solution (formal proof) verified with examples or test cases, written up in the Final Research Document (no page limit; submit as comprehensive as needed). Division Requirements & Paper Standards Problem, Definitions & Proofs: State all definitions, assumptions, and notation clearly before presenting lemmas and theorems. Note on Methodology Section: A traditional experimental methodology section may not be required for every pure mathematics project; focus instead on logical steps and rigorous proof. Examples & Test Cases: Verify your general proof or formula on concrete small cases, boundary values, or numerical examples. Distinguishing Prior Work: Cite known theorems in the References section and state clearly what new angle, proof, or model your team developed. Evaluation Focus & Sample Project Topics What Mentors & Judges Check: Logical accuracy of the proof, clarity of mathematical writing, and how well small examples or counterexamples are analyzed. Sample Topic Area 1: Number theory problems, divisibility properties, modular arithmetic patterns, or Diophantine equations. Sample Topic Area 2: Combinatorial counting problems, invariants, graph properties, or geometric inequalities. Sample Topic Area 3: Mathematical modeling of real-world processes (traffic flow, queuing, epidemiology, or optimization). Student Registration (AZ) Student Registration (EN / International) Track 02 · Computer Science & Artificial Intelligence ← All Divisions Comparison Computer Science & AI Covers software engineering, algorithms, data analysis, and artificial intelligence projects where teams build working code, test it on real datasets, and compare measurable results against baseline methods. Machine Learning & Deep Learning Computer Vision & Medical Imaging NLP & Azerbaijani Language Models Algorithms & Data Structures Cybersecurity & Cryptography Computational Bioinformatics Stage 1 · Nov 15 – 28 1-Page Project Plan Problem statement (3–4 sentences), research question, planned algorithm or model architecture, data sources, and expected software output. Stage 2 · By Dec 19 (Mid-Term) Working Code & Real-Data Tests Working initial code and preliminary tests conducted on real data, demonstrated during the 3-minute Mid-Term review with mentors. Stage 3 · By Dec 26 (Final Paper) Tested Software & Baseline Comparison Tested functional software, measurable quantitative results, comparison with baseline indicators, and the Final Research Paper (no page limit). Division Requirements & Paper Standards Working Code on Real Data: Projects must include functional code tested on real datasets—not just a theoretical concept or an unvalidated interface. Mandatory Baseline Comparison: Measure your software or model using clear metrics (accuracy, F1-score, error rate, runtime) and compare it directly against a baseline method. Code & Appendices Rule: Include key result tables and charts in the main paper; long code blocks and large data tables may be placed in the Appendices section (no page limit). AI Tool Declaration & Honest Failure Analysis: Transparently declare any AI assistants (ChatGPT, Claude, Copilot) used for coding or editing, and explain what did not work during testing. Evaluation Focus & Sample Project Topics What Mentors & Judges Check: Reliability of the code, quality of the dataset and baseline comparison, and practical usefulness of the software. Sample Topic Area 1: Computer vision or image classification for agriculture, medical screening, document reading, or environmental monitoring. Sample Topic Area 2: Natural language processing tools, datasets, or educational software. Sample Topic Area 3: Optimization algorithms, cybersecurity & anomaly detection tools, or accessible assistive software. Student Registration (AZ) Student Registration (EN / International) Track 03 · Engineering & Physical Sciences ← All Divisions Comparison Engineering & Physics Covers applied physics and engineering projects where students design and build an experimental apparatus or physical prototype, take real measurements, and compare empirical data against their initial target. Mechanical & Structural Engineering Energy, Thermodynamics & Renewables Aerodynamics & Fluid Mechanics Optics, Acoustics & Condensed Matter Materials Science & Nanomaterials Environmental & Filtration Engineering Stage 1 · Nov 15 – 28 1-Page Project Plan Engineering or physics problem, objective or hypothesis, sequential construction and testing steps, required materials/instruments, and target outcome. Stage 2 · By Dec 19 (Mid-Term) Initial Prototype & First Measurements Initial prototype or experimental apparatus built with preliminary measurements already taken and presented at the Mid-Term review. Stage 3 · By Dec 26 (Final Paper) Functional Prototype & Data Comparison Working prototype, systematic comparison of obtained empirical data with the initial objective, analysis of limitations, and Final Research Paper (no page limit). Division Requirements & Paper Standards Physical Prototype or Apparatus: Purely theoretical descriptions or CAD drawings without experimental measurements are not sufficient; teams must build and test a physical setup. Empirical Measurements & Units: Record quantitative measurements in clear tables and graphs with proper units. Comparison with Initial Target: Directly compare your measured data against the target stated in your Stage 1 Project Plan. Honest Explanation of Failures: Document mechanical, material, or measurement limitations and explain why certain trials did not work as expected. Evaluation Focus & Sample Project Topics What Mentors & Judges Check: Sound application of physics/engineering principles, repeatable measurements, and a functional prototype demonstrated at the Baku stand or live during the Pathway 2 online oral defense. Sample Topic Area 1: Renewable energy prototypes (solar panel efficiency, wind turbine blade design, thermal insulation, energy harvesting). Sample Topic Area 2: Water filtration, air purification, or biodegradable/composite material strength testing. Sample Topic Area 3: Experimental physics setups investigating aerodynamics, acoustics, optics, or vibration damping. Student Registration (AZ) Student Registration (EN / International) Track 04 · Natural Sciences (Biology & Chemistry) ← All Divisions Comparison Natural Sciences (Biology & Chemistry) Covers experimental biology, chemistry, biochemistry, and environmental science projects based on controlled laboratory or field experiments, repeated trials alongside a control group, and evidence-based conclusions. Molecular & Cellular Biology Biochemistry & Enzymology Organic, Inorganic & Green Chemistry Ecology & Environmental Remediation Plant Sciences & Agritech Analytical & Food Chemistry Stage 1 · Nov 15 – 28 1-Page Project Plan Biological or chemical problem, testable hypothesis or research question, step-by-step experimental method, materials/reagents, and safety plan. Stage 2 · By Dec 19 (Mid-Term) Experimental Setup & Initial Trial Experimental setup established and initial trial conducted, with preliminary observations presented at the Mid-Term mentor review. Stage 3 · By Dec 26 (Final Paper) Repeated Trials & Control Group Repeated trials conducted alongside a control group, clear scientific conclusion, analysis of failed trials, and Final Research Paper (no page limit). Division Requirements & Paper Standards Mandatory Control Group: Every experiment must include a control group so that your test variable can be compared against a baseline under identical conditions. Repeated Trials: Conduct repeated trials rather than relying on a single run, and record all numeric measurements, tables, graphs, and photographs. Reproducible Methodology: Write your Methodology 