      "evidenceText": "US Sentences Venezuelan National to 12 Years for Laundering Over $1 Billion | OCCRP Money Laundering • United States US Sentences Venezuelan National to 12 Years for Laundering Over $1 Billion November 4, 2026 | 0 min read Share Article &times; Aa Text Settings &times; Text Size Small Medium Large Line Spacing Compact Normal Spacious A U.S. district court sentenced a 60-year-old Venezuelan national Jorge Figueira to 12 years in prison and ordered him to surrender $1 billion for running a money laundering network tied to drug trafficking organizations, according to a Tuesday statement . Investigators found that Figueira's operation moved over $1 billion through a single cryptocurrency wallet and various financial accounts. The network funneled the illicit funds into and out of the U.S. using shell companies, traditional bank accounts, and cryptocurrency exchanges, directing outbound money to individuals and businesses worldwide, including in Colombia, China, Panama, and Mexico. November 4, 2026 Share Article: Related Articles 2 days ago Five Questions About the Future of Global Anticorruption Efforts in the Trump Era Power and Influence United States 2 days ago US Prosecutors Ask Judge to Allow Bribery Evidence in South American Drug Trafficking Case Organized Crime United States 2 weeks ago EXPLAINER Defanging Laws and Bringing Home Billions: How Trump Has Rewritten the Corruption Rulebook At Home and Abroad Corruption United States 2 days ago Five Questions About the Future of Global Anticorruption Efforts in the Trump Era Power and Influence United States 2 days ago US Prosecutors Ask Judge to Allow Bribery Evidence in South American Drug Trafficking Case Organized Crime United States 2 weeks ago EXPLAINER Defanging Laws and Bringing Home Billions: How Trump Has Rewritten the Corruption Rulebook At Home and Abroad Corruption United States Help us improve the website! Click below to give us input. It’ll only take 5 minutes. 👉 Help OCCRP Don't