      "evidenceText": "Poynter’s MediaWise launches global initiative pairing newsrooms with independent creators - Poynter Poynter’s MediaWise launches global initiative pairing newsrooms with independent creators - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Media Literacy MediaWise News Releases AI and tech entrepreneur Alan Daitch, left, Patricio Carson, head of digital news at Todo Noticias, center, and Brittani Kollar, deputy director of MediaWise, right, meet in Argentina. (Photo Credit: Todo Noticias) By: Adele Streissguth October 29, 2026 ST. PETERSBURG, Fla. (Oct 29, 2026) — Around the world, more than a quarter of people now get at least some of their information from creators or influencers focused on current events. As more audiences turn to them alongside traditional news organizations, newsrooms and creators are increasingly reaching the same people. To explore how they can work together, Poynter’s MediaWise , in partnership with Microsoft , announced the Creator + Newsroom Studio at Media Party in Buenos Aires, Argentina. The global initiative pairs five news organizations with independent content creators to co-produce social-first journalism by testing new approaches in producing and distributing news, and exploring AI literacy. Participating organizations include Chicago Public Media in the United States, Todo Noticias in Argentina, Africa Uncensored in Kenya, Indigenous Community Television in Australia and Augsburger Allgemeine with Guenter Holland School of Journalism in Germany . At Media Party, MediaWise’s deputy director Brittani Kollar and Todo Noticias’ head of social media Lara Krynveniuk led a joint workshop gathering perspectives from journalists and creators about their firsthand experiences working together. Insights from the workshop and lessons from the initiative will shape a global toolkit to help other newsrooms and creators collaborate. The toolkit will be available in Spring 2027. “Creators and newsrooms each have something the other needs. Creators have built direct relationships with their audiences, and newsrooms bring deep expertise in reporting and editorial standards,” said Kollar. “We want to see what happens when they work side by side, including both successes and pain points, to ensure we share practical guidance.” Kollar is co-leading the initiative with independent news creator and MediaWise ambassador Chris Vazquez . Vazquez worked in traditional newsrooms before branching out on his own as a news content creator. His experience represents a deep understanding of what both newsrooms and creators face. “These collaborations provide unique opportunities to share rigorous, principled journalism in formats that reach people effectively,” Vazquez said. “Mapping out how to set those partnerships up for success will only make our civic information ecosystem stronger.” Each newsroom-creator pair will develop and publish social videos together and document what they learn about editorial standards, audience engagement, emerging technologies and the challenges of working across different workflows. MediaWise will evaluate the partnerships throughout the year, tracking how the relationships develop and what participants learn from one another. Participants will also attend workshops on artificial intelligence and media literacy and complete AI audits, assessing how they currently use and understand the technology. Research will also examine audience response to the published videos. Participants in focus groups will complete a survey about their news consumption and AI literacy before viewing the videos and providing feedback, including their perceptions of the content’s trustworthiness. The initiative is part of a broader Microsoft-funded project that also includes the development of a Media and Information Literacy Handbook Supporting Content Creators in the Age of Artificial Intelligence by UNESCO. The handbook aims to strengthen skills in a context where content creation, curation and distribution are increasingly shaped by AI. The two initiatives are highly complementary, and UNESCO and Poynter are actively sharing expertise, insights and lessons learned across both workstreams. “The way people find and evaluate information is changing rapidly, shaped by creators, news organizations and AI. Through our work with Poynter and UNESCO, we’re combining complementary expertise to develop practical tools, strengthen AI and media literacy, and help people navigate today’s information environment with greater confidence,” said Ginny Badanes, General Manager, Tech for Society at Microsoft. UNESCO held the first Handbook consultation workshop, co-facilitated by Microsoft, during Global Media and Information Literacy Week in Nairobi, Kenya on 26-27 October 2026. The workshop brought together content creators, representatives of creators’ networks and associations, and other relevant stakeholders to gather broad and diverse feedback on the Handbook draft. The feedback collected will inform the next iteration of the Handbook’s development, with its official launch scheduled for June 2027. “This is just the beginning of the Creator + Newsroom Studio at Poynter. As audience needs and the news and information ecosystem evolves, this Studio will provide support to creators, newsrooms and their collaborations,” said Kollar. Media Contact Jennifer Orsi Vice President, Publishing and Local News Initiatives The Poynter Institute jorsi@poynter.org About the Poynter Institute The Poynter Institute is a global nonprofit working to address society’s most pressing issues by teaching journalists and journalism, covering the media and the complexities facing the industry, convening and community building, improving the capacity and sustainability of news organizations and fostering trust and reliability of information. The Institute is the gold standard in journalistic excellence and dedicated to the preservation and advancement of press freedom in democracies worldwide. Through Poynter, journalists, newsrooms, businesses, big tech corporations and citizens convene to find solutions that promote trust and transparency in news and stoke meaningful public discourse. The world’s top journalists and emerging media leaders rely on the Institute to learn new skills, adopt best practices, better serve audiences, scale operations and improve the quality of the universally shared information ecosystem. The Newmark Ethics Center, the International Fact-Checking Network (IFCN), MediaWise and PolitiFact are all members of the Poynter organization. Support for Poynter and our entities upholds the integrity of the free press and the U.S. First Amendment and builds public confidence in journalism and media — an essential for healthy democracies. Learn more at poynter.org . About MediaWise MediaWise is a media literacy initiative of the nonprofit Poynter Institute. The program teaches people of all ages and backgrounds how to responsibly engage with online content in the age of information overload. MediaWise was created in 2018 to empower citizens to find trusted sources and make sense of the vast amount of information at their fingertips. MediaWise brings simple, yet effective, media literacy tools to people where they are — whether they’re on Facebook, Instagram, YouTube or TikTok — and walk them through every step to determine what’s real and what’s not on the internet. Through MediaWise, everyone can do their part to reduce the spread of misinformation and elevate the facts. Follow MediaWise on social media to learn how to debunk viral claims and be more critical consumers of online information. Explore programs and be part of the solution at poynter.org/mediawise . This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE Tags: MediaWise , Microsoft Adele Streissguth Adele Streissguth serves as the Marketing and Communications Lead at The Poynter Institute. Prior to this role, she worked as a copywriter at a marketing&hellip; Adele Streissguth After standing up for his ‘60 Minutes’ colleagues, Scott Pelley wants to explore the ‘triumph of truth’ ‘When autocrats around the world and through history attempt to take away all of the people's rights, they always start with the press’ November 15, 2026 Angela Fu Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom Congressional investigations could put the administration’s attacks on the press under scrutiny, while bipartisan legislation offers a path to reform November 13, 2026 Angela Fu Opinion | Longtime NBC Sports announcer Dan Hicks, dies suddenly. He was 64 The longtime announcer and host was a staple in sports broadcasting, known for his voice, easy manner and mentorship November 13, 2026 Tom Jones The local newsroom is disappearing. The news could pay the price. As newspapers abandon their buildings, they risk losing the mentoring, institutional knowledge and presence that help make good reporting possible November 13, 2026 Jackie Spinner Scott Pelley says CBS has lost its credibility. A post-midterms about-face wouldn’t help. ‘It’s not a question of which way the bias leans. The problem is that editorial control was taken from journalists’ November 12, 2026 Angela Fu Back to News Become a Poynter Member Don't miss out on a year of learning. JOIN TODAY Already a Member? Log in Start your day informed and inspired. Subscribe Get the Poynter newsletter that's right for you. Media Jobs News Editor Director of Advertising DIGITAL SALES STRATEGIST BROADCAST JOURNALIST: ARTS & CULTURE Deputy Managing Editor- News Director of Technology Chair and Professor of the Practice Publisher and Chief Executive Officer - Connecticut Mirror / CT Mirror News Editor, The Stranger - Seattle, WA (98122) Group Editor - Columbia, MD (21045) Search Media Jobs Advertise a job &times; This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE",
